<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Ventris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Ventris was an English architect and amateur linguist who deciphered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, the script of the Mycenaean palaces. In 1952, working from the tablets found at Pylos and Knossos, he showed that it recorded an early form of Greek, and with John Chadwick he published Documents in Mycenaean Greek in 1956. He was killed in a car accident the same year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-ventris.jpg" alt="Michael Ventris" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michael_Ventris_obituary_photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
